  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain-free conditions will persist through the \nend of October and possibly the first day of November \n\nAn area of high pressure will settle south across the Carolinas\nand southern Appalachians today, allowing a weak/dry cold front\nto drift southeast across the District this afternoon and \nevening. Outside of a few sprinkles across the Northern \nMountains later today or early tonight, no measurable rainfall \nis expected this entire week.\n\nIn the wake of the frontal passage, another, larger area of \nhigh pressure will build Southeast across the Upper Great Lakes \nand extend our lengthy string of rainfree conditions through \npart or all of this upcoming weekend.\n\nLocally dense large stream/river valley fog will occur across \ncentral PA and the Susq valley early today, while light wind \nand/or some mid and high clouds has limited much in the way of \nfog development (so far) across the Northern Mtns and Laurel \nHighlands where temp/dewpt spreads are between 1-6 deg F. Will \nkeep the mention of patchy fog for areas outside of the Susq \nMainstem and its West Branch Valley.\n \nModerate variations will occur in daily and nocturnal \ntemperatures as at least one weak, dry cold fronts moves SE \nacross the Commonwealth during the upcoming 5-6 days. Wind \ndirection/speed and cloud cover will also show some minor day- \nday variations. \n\nThe first of the aforementioned dry cold fronts will slip SE \nacross the CWA late today/early tonight, but with only limited \nmoisture to work with, just some periods of BKN, shallow strato \ncu and cirrus will occur with no rainfall expected beyond a few \nsprinkles over the Northern Mtns tonight. \n\nHave kept the PoPs below 5% for Mon night and that's probably \nstretching it on the safe side. \n\nAfter a brief shot of seasonably cool air Tuesday and Wed \n(directed mainly at the NE half of the state), a late \nweek/Saturday rebound in temps (to well above normal values in \nthe low-mid 60s across the Mtns and upper 60s to mid 70s in the \nCentral and Southern Valleys) will follow. A second, weak and \nessentially dry frontal boundary could brush by across the \nNorthern tier counties Friday afternoon and night. \n\nThe next chance for any measurable rain is Sunday \nafternoon/evening.",
